window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-11","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-11-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-01/","title_he":"א – מכירת תשמישי פולחן, איסור 'לפני עיוור","title_en":"Selling Items Used for Worship; the Prohibition of Lifnei Iver","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to cause a person to stumble into a transgression, whether a Jew or a gentile, as it is stated: \"You shall not place a stumbling block before the blind; you shall fear your God, I am the Lord\" (Vayikra 19:14). That is, just as it is forbidden to place before a blind person an obstacle that will injure his body, so it is forbidden to place before a person an obstacle that will cause him to stumble into a transgression that will injure his soul (Avoda Zara 6a; Ran ad loc.). Therefore, it is forbidden to sell a gentile items needed for idolatry, such as vessels used for worship or a white rooster, which they customarily sacrificed to idols. Even when the gentile does not say that he intends to sacrifice the rooster to an idol, as long as it is reasonable to assume that this is his intention, for example if he insists that it be white, it is forbidden to sell it to him (Avoda Zara 13b; SA YD 151:1).</p><p>Some say that even when the gentile can buy his items of worship from others, it is rabbinically forbidden to sell them to him, because the seller thereby assists him in committing a transgression. Others say that the prohibition of lifnei iver applies only when without the Jew the gentile would be unable to commit the transgression, but if he can buy his items of worship from another gentile, there is no prohibition for the Jew to sell them to him, since in any case the gentile will commit the transgression. In practice, in a time of need the custom is to be lenient, and those who are scrupulous try to avoid it (Rema YD 151:1).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The source for the prohibition of causing a gentile to stumble because of lifnei iver is in Tractate Avoda Zara 6a-b: \"It was taught: Rabbi Natan said: From where do we know that a person may not hand a cup of wine to a nazirite, or a limb from a living animal to the descendants of Noah? The verse states: 'You shall not place a stumbling block before the blind' (Vayikra 19:14).\" It is explained there in the Gemara that the prohibition of lifnei iver applies when without him the transgression would not have been carried out, for example when the nazirite is on one side of the river and the wine is on the other side, and only by handing it to the nazirite can he drink it; in that case the one who hands it is considered one who \"causes to stumble.\" Similarly, it is explained there in the Gemara that because of the prohibition of lifnei iver it is forbidden to sell a gentile an animal for the purpose of sacrificing it to an idol. And so too they ruled in the Mishna (Avoda Zara 13b) that it is forbidden to sell a gentile things that are known to be used for idolatry, such as a white rooster, which they customarily sacrificed.<br>On the simple reading, the prohibition of lifnei iver with regard to Noahides is by Torah law, since the baraita equated it with the prohibition of lifnei iver with regard to a Jew. So writes the Ritva (Avoda Zara 6a); Sdei Ḥemed (letter vav 26:16). This is also evident from Rashi (Avoda Zara 6a, s.v. \"Ve-lifnei\") and from the Bekhor Shor on Pesaḥim 22b. However, Responsa Pnei Yehoshua (YD 3) proposed a novel view that lifnei iver with regard to Noahides is rabbinically forbidden, for the Sages said (Avoda Zara 2b) that since the gentiles did not keep the seven Noahide commandments, the Holy One, blessed be He, arose and released them from them; only a Jew is rabbinically forbidden to cause them to stumble in this. The Yad Efrayim (151:1) cites him. However, many Aḥaronim rejected his words and wrote that the prohibition of lifnei iver with regard to a gentile is by Torah law (Ḥatam Sofer vol. 5, 185; Imrei Bina vol. 1, YD Treifot 7; Beit Yehuda YD 17; Beit She'arim YD 227).<br>The Taz (151:1) writes that where it is doubtful whether the gentile is buying the item for idolatry, we are lenient. End quote. This is as we learned (Mishna Shevi'it 5:8-9; Gemara Avoda Zara 15b) that it is permitted to lend agricultural tools to one suspected of violating Shevi'it when one can assume that he will use them for a permitted purpose. Similarly, in Nedarim 62b, Rav Ashi was permitted to sell wood from his grove to fire-worshippers, even though most likely they would use it for their worship. As the Ran writes: \"Wherever one can assume [a permitted use], we assume it,\" and so writes the Rosh.<br>Some say that the prohibition of selling items of idolatry to a gentile applies only when the gentile has no possibility of buying them elsewhere, but if he can buy them elsewhere, even though it is clear that the gentile is buying for idolatry, there is no prohibition (Mordekhai Avoda Zara 795, based on the Ravya in the name of his father, R. Yoel Ha-Levi; Tosafot Avoda Zara 6b, s.v. \"Minayin\"; Semag, negative commandments 168; Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Toledot Adam Ve-Ḥava 17:6; Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona Avoda Zara 6b). Others say that it is rabbinically forbidden even when he can buy them elsewhere, because of assisting transgressors (Tosafot Shabbat 3a, s.v. \"Baba\"; Rosh ad loc. 1:1; Hagahot Mordekhai Shabbat 450; Ran Avoda Zara 1b in the Rif pagination, s.v. \"Minayin\"). The Rema in Darkhei Moshe summarised the dispute and wrote that the custom is to be lenient, and a ba'al nefesh (a spiritually scrupulous person) should be stringent with himself. The Shakh (151:6) was lenient, and explained that this is the view of all the Rishonim: there is no prohibition to sell items of idolatry to a gentile or to an apostate Jew when he can buy them from others, since a Jew is not commanded to keep them away from prohibition. And all that they wrote to forbid concerns selling in this manner to a Jew: since one is commanded to rebuke him and keep him away from prohibition, it is also forbidden to assist him in committing a transgression. End quote."}]},{"slug":"20-11-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-02/","title_he":"ב – איסור 'לפני עיוור' במכירה לסוחרים","title_en":"The Prohibition of Lifnei Iver in Selling to Merchants","body_en":"<p>It is permitted to sell a gentile merchant large quantities of items for him to sell to other people who will likely use them for idolatry, such as roosters that they customarily sacrifice to idols. For the obligation placed upon us is not to cause the gentile before us to stumble, as it is stated: \"You shall not place a stumbling block before the blind,\" but we bear no responsibility to ensure that the one who buys from us will not cause other gentiles to stumble; this is called lifnei de-lifnei (\"before the one who is before\") (Avoda Zara 14a; SA YD 151:1).</p><p>But when it is likely that the merchant who bought from us will thereby cause Jews to stumble, it is forbidden to sell to him, for the responsibility placed upon us toward our Jewish brethren is greater, and even lifnei de-lifnei is forbidden (Rosh; Taz 151:3). Therefore, it is forbidden to sell non-kosher foods to a merchant when one might mistakenly think that they are kosher and a Jew might stumble on them.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In Avoda Zara 14a it is explained that there is no prohibition of lifnei de-lifnei in selling to a gentile. However, when there is concern that the gentile will sell the forbidden item to a Jew, because it is not apparent that it is forbidden, such as a garment containing sha'atnez that is not discernible, or wheat that became ḥametz before Pesaḥ, it is forbidden to sell them to the gentile lest he sell them to a Jew (Pesaḥim 40b; Nidda 61b). The prohibition applies only when the Jew cannot recognise it, because the sha'atnez is lost within the garment and the leavening is not discernible in the wheat (Tosafot Rid ad loc. 14a, s.v. \"Amar\"). The Rishonim explained that the prohibition is not because of lifnei iver, since there is no prohibition of lifnei de-lifnei in selling to a gentile; rather, the prohibition is because he assists in causing the Jew to stumble into a transgression (Tosafot Avoda Zara 15b, s.v. \"Le-oved\"; Rosh ad loc. 1:14; Ritva, Meiri and Nimukei Yosef ad loc. 14a, and others). And so writes the Taz (151:3) as halakha.<br>Some say that the permission of lifnei de-lifnei in selling to a gentile applies when it is not certain that the item being sold will serve idolatry, but when this is certain, it is not considered lifnei de-lifnei and it is forbidden (Tosafot Avoda Zara 14b, s.v. \"Ḥatzav\"; Semag, negative commandments 45). Others say that even when it is certain, it is considered lifnei de-lifnei and is permitted (Terumat Ha-deshen, Pesakim U-khetavim 27). The Rema (YD 139:15) cites both opinions and concludes that one who is stringent will be blessed.<br><b>Lifnei de-lifnei through an agent</b>: According to those who hold that Noahides were commanded regarding castration, it is forbidden to sell a gentile an animal that he intends to castrate; but when it is known that the gentile buyer will not castrate it himself but will instruct his servant to castrate it, the Terumat Ha-deshen (299) inclined to permit, because this is lifnei de-lifnei. And although the servant is his agent, there is no agency for a transgression, and there is also no agency for a gentile. End quote. So writes the Beit Shmuel (EH 5:19). On the other hand, according to the Mas'at Binyamin (97) there is agency for a gentile, and according to the Ḥavot Ya'ir (136) there is agency for a gentile where it leads to stringency; therefore this is not considered lifnei de-lifnei, and it is forbidden."}]},{"slug":"20-11-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-03/","title_he":"ג – איסור שותפות עם הרגיל להישבע באליליו","title_en":"The Prohibition of Partnership with One Who Customarily Swears by His Idols","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to cause a gentile to swear or vow in the name of idols, as it is stated: \"Make no mention of the name of other gods; it shall not be heard through your mouth\" (Shemot 23:13). \"It shall not be heard through your mouth\" means that one should also not cause others to swear by their idols. In order not to come to this prohibition, the Sages forbade entering into a partnership with an idolater, because such a partnership is based on an understanding that if they come to a dispute, the gentile will swear by his idols, and he will not lie, since he honours them (Sanhedrin 63b; SA ḤM 176:51).</p><p>However, when a monetary dispute has broken out between a Jew and an idolater, and only if the Jew demands that he swear might he admit the truth, according to many it is permitted for the Jew to demand that the gentile swear in order to try to save his money. For in a case of dispute, the prohibition of making him swear is rabbinic, and therefore in a pressing situation, to prevent a loss, it is permitted to make him swear. Others say that it is forbidden by Torah law to make him swear by his idols, and therefore even in a pressing situation it is forbidden to make him swear.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Sanhedrin 63b: \"Shmuel's father said: It is forbidden for a person to enter into a partnership with a gentile (an idolater), lest he become obligated to take an oath to him and he swear by his idol, and the Torah said: 'it shall not be heard through your mouth.'\" Some say that the prohibition of making him swear is by Torah law, and therefore the Rashbam and the Yad Rama forbade making him swear even where there would be a loss, as the Nimukei Yosef explained (Sanhedrin 16b in the Rif pagination). This is also implied by the Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 5:11; Sefer Ha-mitzvot, negative commandment 14), except that one does not receive lashes for it.<br>According to many, the prohibition is rabbinic; this is the view of the Rif, Rid, Or Zaru'a, Semak, Rashba and others. Therefore, in a pressing situation, in order to save money, it is permitted to make a gentile swear by his idols. This is the view of Rabbeinu Tam in Tosafot (Gittin 50b, s.v. \"Yetomin\"; Sanhedrin 63b, s.v. \"She-lo\"); Rosh (Sanhedrin 7:3); Rabbeinu Yeruḥam; SA ḤM 176:51. So writes the Tashbetz Katan (520) and the Radbaz (Ha-ḥadashot 1:166), who explained that the verse is an asmakhta (a scriptural support for a rabbinic law). This is difficult: if the prohibition is rabbinic, why did the Sages make a fence around it and forbid entering into a partnership with a gentile lest one need to make him swear? One may answer that entering into a partnership itself rests on the understanding that in a dispute the gentile will swear by his idols, as the Rosh writes (Bekhorot 1:1), that \"it is likely to come to an oath in the end.\" And since the Jew respects the reliability of his oath, it turns out that already when entering into the partnership the Jew pays honour to the idols and transgresses a prohibition.<br>Some say that there is no prohibition to enter into a partnership with a gentile and to make him swear, but only a measure of piety, for in Megilla 28a an Amora is cited as saying: \"Let [a reward] come to me, for I never entered into a partnership with a gentile\" (happy am I that I never entered into a partnership with a gentile idolater). This implies that it is a measure of piety, for there is no reason to praise oneself for observing an established halakha. So writes the Ra'ah in the name of the Ramban (Avoda Zara 21b), and the Ritva (Megilla 28a). This reasoning is mentioned by several Rishonim. Even Rabbeinu Tam relied on it in support of permitting, in a pressing situation, to make a gentile swear (Tosafot Bekhorot 2b, s.v. \"Shema\"). However, in practice, according to most Rishonim it is rabbinically forbidden to make a gentile swear by his idols and to enter into a partnership with him; and the reason that Tractate Megilla speaks of a measure of piety is that it refers to gentiles who do not customarily swear by idols (Rashba), or to a case where they agreed between them that even if they come to a dispute the Jew will not demand that the gentile swear (see Tosafot).<br>Although according to most poskim it is permitted in a pressing situation to make the gentile swear by his idols, the Rosh (Responsa, kelal 18:11) writes that if he sees that the gentile is ready to swear, it is better to forgo, for what is the benefit of making him swear if in any case he will not save his money thereby.<br><b>Partnership with Christians:</b> With Christians they permitted doing business on the basis of three reasons: <b>a)</b> Christianity is idolatry with shituf (association of other beings with God), and Noahides are not forbidden to swear by it (Rabbeinu Tam in Tosafot Sanhedrin 63b, s.v. \"Asur\"; Rosh ad loc. 7:3; Agudda and R. Yehuda Almandari ad loc.; Semag, positive commandments 82; Hagahot Maimoniyot, Hilkhot Sheluḥin 5:10; Rabbeinu Yeruḥam). <b>b)</b> Many Christians swear in the name of God without mentioning \"that man\" (Semak 119; Tashbetz Katan 520; Responsa Rashba 7:524; Ran and Meiri on Sanhedrin 63b; Semak of Zurich 118:60). <b>c)</b> Many swear in the name of their saints, whom they do not regard as gods (Ri; Riaz; Maharam of Rothenburg 97; Rabbeinu Yona and R. Yehonatan of Lunel on Sanhedrin ad loc.; Responsa Rashba 7:302; Rosh Bekhorot 1:1; Orḥot Ḥayim; Ohel Mo'ed)."}]},{"slug":"20-11-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-04/","title_he":"ד – איסור משא ומתן בימי אידיהם (חגיהם) ולפניהם","title_en":"The Prohibition of Doing Business on Their Festival Days and Before Them","body_en":"<p>The Sages forbade doing business with an idolater on his eid (his festival day), because out of the sense of satisfaction at his success in business he will likely give thanks for it to his idol on his festival day, and it turns out that the Jew caused this and transgressed what is stated: \"Make no mention of the name of other gods; it shall not be heard through your mouth\" (Shemot 23:13). The Sages also forbade doing business with them during the three days before their festival, because on those days they used to prepare the sacrifices they offered to the idol on the festival day, and any success that came their way on those days they believed came to them by virtue of the idol, which looked favourably upon the sacrifice they were preparing for it, and therefore they gave thanks to it for this. But four days before their festival they did not usually prepare the festival sacrifice, and therefore there is no concern that the idolater will connect his success with his acts of worship.</p><p>Included in the prohibition of doing business are selling or buying, lending or borrowing an object, lending or borrowing money, and collecting a loan or repaying it, for each of these activities can arouse a sense of satisfaction in the idolater, and if it takes place on his festival day, or on the days in which he prepares his worship for his festival day, he will likely give thanks for it to his idol.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"The Sages called the festival day of idolaters \"yom eidam,\" a term denoting calamity and ruin, as part of the mitzva to destroy their name and change it into a derogatory one (below 14:1). Avoda Zara 2a, in the Mishna: \"For three days before the festivals of idolaters it is forbidden to do business with them, to lend objects to them or borrow objects from them, to lend money to them or borrow money from them, to repay them or to collect repayment from them.\" The reason is explained there in the Gemara (5b): on these days too they would give thanks to the idols for their success, since on them they prepared the animals that they sacrificed on their festival, and the names of their idols were constantly on their lips (Tosafot ad loc., s.v. \"U-mi ba'inan\"). In addition, they believed that the idol saw their efforts to appease it by preparing the sacrifice and helped them, and they thanked it for this on their festival day (Ra'ah, Rosh and Ran). It turns out that the Jew caused the gentile to give thanks to his idols, and transgressed what is stated: \"Make no mention of the name of other gods; it shall not be heard through your mouth\" (Shemot 23:13) (Rashi 6a).<br>The Gemara (6a) raises a doubt: \"They asked: Is it because of profit, or perhaps because of 'You shall not place a stumbling block before the blind'? What is the practical difference? Where he has an animal of his own: if you say it is because of profit, he is giving him profit; if you say it is because of 'You shall not place a stumbling block before the blind,' he has one of his own.\" According to most Rishonim, the doubt is as follows: Are the prohibitions in the Mishna \"because of profit,\" as explained above, that following his success he will give thanks to the idol, and the Jew thereby transgresses \"it shall not be heard through your mouth,\" but there is no prohibition of lifnei iver here, since the gentile can give thanks to his idols even without this (Taz 148:3)? Or are the prohibitions of the Mishna because of lifnei iver, for example when the gentile has no animal to sacrifice to the idol and the Jew sells him one, or when he has no money to buy his items of worship and through the business with the Jew he will have money to buy them (Tosafot 6a, s.v. \"O\"); but if the gentile has all his items of worship, there is no prohibition at all? From the course of the sugya (6b) we learn that, as halakha, the prohibition is \"because of profit,\" as explained in the first paragraph (Rashi 2a, Rif, Rambam, Ran, Meiri, Taz 148:1; Shakh 1).<br>However, Rabbeinu Tam (Tosafot 2a, s.v. \"Asur\") has a different approach. Regarding the prohibition of lending objects, lending money and repayment, Rabbeinu Tam too agrees that the prohibition is because of \"profit,\" that is, lest he give thanks to the idol. But ordinary buying and selling is permitted in his opinion even on their festival day, for since the gentile pays for the purchase, this is an activity that produces no sense of success, and there is no concern that he will give thanks for it to the idol. And the Gemara's question (6a) whether the prohibition is because of \"profit\" or because of lifnei iver concerns buying and selling items of worship: since items of worship are involved, perhaps even when he can obtain his items of worship from another person there is concern that he will give thanks to the idol for it; or perhaps the prohibition is only because of lifnei iver, and when the gentile can obtain his items of worship from another person it is permitted to sell to him.<br>The Ramban and Ra'ah also hold, like Rabbeinu Tam, that the Gemara's question concerns only buying and selling, but in their opinion the question concerns all buying and selling, even of things unrelated to worship: whether, despite the payment, buying and selling also produces a sense of profit on account of which he will give thanks to the idol, as with lending objects, lending money and repayment; or whether it produces no sense of profit, and then the prohibition is only because of lifnei iver, in selling items of worship that the gentile cannot obtain elsewhere. And since the doubt concerns a rabbinic law, the Ramban and Ra'ah ruled leniently, that the prohibition in buying and selling applies only to selling items of worship, and because of lifnei iver. In summary, according to Rabbeinu Tam, the Ramban and the Ra'ah, there is no prohibition at all on doing business in anything that is not an item of worship."}]},{"slug":"20-11-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-05/","title_he":"ה – ההיתר למעשה","title_en":"The Permission in Practice","body_en":"<p>The Sages ruled that outside the Land of Israel it is permitted to do business with idolaters during the three days before their festival, and only on their festival day itself is it forbidden (Avoda Zara 11b). This is because the Sages saw that idolaters had become less devout, and less careful to give thanks to the idols for their successes. Similarly, we learned (Ḥullin 13a-b) that when idolaters used to have in mind, when slaughtering an animal, that the slaughter was for their idols, there were Sages who ruled that all meat slaughtered by an idolater is forbidden for any benefit, like the law of an \"offering to idolatry\" (above 10:2). However, Rabbi Yoḥanan said that this law does not apply outside the Land, because \"gentiles outside the Land are not idolaters; rather, they are following the custom of their ancestors,\" and therefore they do not have the idols in mind when they slaughter, and consequently the meat they slaughtered is permitted for benefit.</p><p>In addition, after the destruction of the Second Temple, Israel was scattered among the nations and became dependent on them, and there was concern that if they did not do business with them for many days, they would lose their source of livelihood, and hostility toward them would also increase. Therefore, they were lenient to do business with them during the three days before their festival. Later, when the Jewish community in the Land weakened, in the Land too the custom was to be lenient during the three days before their festival. In addition, over the years the custom of sacrificing animals to idols declined, and consequently they were not occupied with preparing sacrifices during the three days before their festival, and the concern that they would attribute their success on those three days to the help of the idols diminished.</p><p>And although, in principle, on the basis of the reasons for permission mentioned above it would have been possible to permit doing business even on their festival day, they continued to forbid it, because refraining from business on their festival day alone did not greatly harm livelihood and was not dangerous on account of hostility.</p><p>Over the years, the economic need to do business even on their festival days kept growing. The danger of hostility also grew. At the same time, the idolaters became less devout toward their idols, and many used to associate the Name of Heaven in their thanksgiving, as the Christians did. Therefore, already in the days of the Geonim they ruled leniently to do business with them even on their festival day, and this is the halakha (SA YD 148:12).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"In the Gemara (Avoda Zara 7b; 11b), Shmuel said: \"In the Diaspora, only their festival day itself is forbidden.\" Rashi in both places mentions three reasons: <b>a)</b> \"The idolaters are not so devout in idolatry.\" <b>b)</b> \"We cannot hold ourselves back from doing business with them, for we live among them and our livelihood comes from them.\" <b>c)</b> \"Because of fear,\" that is, concern about hostility. (The first reason is given by: Tosafot 2a, s.v. \"Asur\"; Tosafot Rash; Rosh 1:1; Eshkol, Semag and others. The second and third reasons are given by: R. Yehonatan of Lunel; Piskei Riaz. The third reason is given by: Piskei Rid, Or Zaru'a, Rabbeinu Yehuda Almandari.)<br>In the days of the Geonim the custom was to trade with the gentiles even on their festival day. Several reasons were given for this: <b>a)</b> Livelihood. Rabbeinu Gershom (Responsa Rabbeinu Gershom Me'or Ha-gola 21) writes that the permission to trade with Christians is because of the vital need for a livelihood. So write the Ramban, Ra'avan, Ritva and others. Some were lenient even to prevent a loss, that is, a davar ha-aveid (an irretrievable loss) (Nimukei Yosef, Ohel Mo'ed, Meiri). <b>b)</b> They are not devout. Rabbeinu Gershom further writes there that because of the pressing situation one should permit it also because idolaters outside the Land are not devout, but are following the custom of their ancestors. So write Rashi, Tosafot, Ramban, Ritva, Hagahot Maimoniyot and others. Their words rely on what we learned (Avoda Zara 64b-65a), that Amoraim in Babylonia sent gifts to prominent gentiles (Avidarna and Bar Sheshakh) on their festival day, because they knew that they were not devout in their religion and would not give thanks to the idols for the gift. Therefore, when most people are not devout, there is no prohibition of trading on their festival day. <b>c)</b> Christians are not idolaters. Rabbeinu Gershom in his responsum added regarding Christians that since they are not devout in idolatry, they are not considered idolaters. Similarly writes the Ravya (1051), and the Meiri (Avoda Zara 26a). The Ravya there writes that the permission applies in the Land of Israel too. <b>d)</b> Hostility. The Rishonim wrote that since we live among the gentiles, trade should be permitted even on their festival day because of concern about hostility, just as they permitted (Avoda Zara 26a) a Jewish midwife to deliver the baby of an idolatrous woman for pay because of hostility (Teshuvot Ha-geonim, Ra'avan, Yere'im, Semag, Ran, Rosh, Ritva and others). The Ravya there explains that since it is hard to know what the gentile will take offence at and what not, all trade should be permitted. <b>e)</b> The Rishonim gave an additional reason based on the Yerushalmi (Avoda Zara 1:1), which permitted a Jew to trade with his gentile acquaintance \"because it is like flattery,\" that is, the gentile knows that the relationship between them obliges the Jew to behave courteously toward him and to trade with him, and therefore he does not see it as a positive personal attitude for which he should give thanks to his idols (Tosafot, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, Semag, Terumah, Or Zaru'a, Ramban, Rosh and others; see Knesset Ha-gedola, Hagahot Ha-tur 148:17). <b>f)</b> The approach of Rabbeinu Tam, the Ramban and the Ra'ah (cited at the end of the previous note), according to which the prohibition is to sell items of worship only, while in all other things it is permitted to trade without restriction (the Agudda and Orḥot Ḥayim added their view as a supporting factor).<br>Despite the permission, which is based on various foundations, some Rishonim wrote, on the basis of the Yerushalmi (Demai 4:3), that a God-fearing person who is able to should preferably refrain from trading with them on their festival day (Ra'avan, Yere'im, Ramban, Ra'ah, Ran and others; Rema YD 148:12)."}]},{"slug":"20-11-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-06/","title_he":"ו – האיסור להַנות עבודה זרה","title_en":"The Prohibition of Benefiting Idolatry","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to buy at a fair that was held on the days of an idolatrous festival, at which a tax was paid to the idolatry from every purchase, because it is forbidden to give benefit to idolatry. If one transgressed and bought, the Sages penalised him so that he loses what he bought: if he bought an animal, he must cut off its hooves from the knee joint downward, so that it will be evident that it is forbidden to benefit from it; if he bought garments, he must leave them to rot; if he bought metal vessels, he must throw them into the Dead Sea (Avoda Zara 13a; SA YD 149:3).</p><p>However, it is permitted to take part in a fair at which the regional trade took place, even if it is held on an idolatrous festival day, since its main purpose is general commerce and not worship, and also they did not customarily pay a tax from it to the idolatry. Most fairs in the period of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim were conducted in this way (Rema YD 149:4).</p><p>One whose house was adjacent to a house of idolatry, and the wall between them fell, it is forbidden for him, as a Jew, to rebuild it, because by doing so he also builds the wall of the house of idolatry, and thus gives benefit to it. Rather, he should move back four amot and build the wall of his house, so that they will not be able to use his wall as a wall for themselves. And so that they will not be able to benefit from the space between his house and the house of idolatry, he should use those four amot for a lavatory or other degrading things, so that no benefit will accrue to them from it (Avoda Zara 47a-b).<sup>6</sup></p><p>Likewise, it is forbidden to sell land for a house of idolatry to be built on it, because by doing so one gives benefit to idolatry and also assists in its construction. When there is a great need, it is permitted outside the Land to sell the land to a gentile broker, who will sell the land to whomever he wishes.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"In the Mishna, Avoda Zara 12b: \"A city in which there is idolatry, and in it there were shops that were decorated and shops that were not decorated – this was an incident in Beit She'an – and the Sages said: The decorated ones are forbidden, and those that are not decorated are permitted.\" The decorated ones are forbidden because from every purchase in them a certain percentage was set aside as a tax for the idolatry. Rabbi Yoḥanan explained (ibid. 12b-13a) that this is because one thereby gives benefit to the idolatry through the tax, and the decoration served to mark the shops from which this tax was set aside. However, according to Reish Lakish, giving benefit to idolatry is permitted, and the prohibition is because the buyer benefits from the fragrance of the decoration, which had previously been presented as an offering to idolatry. The Rishonim explained (Tosafot, Rashba and Ritva) that he means that giving a small benefit is permitted, but even Reish Lakish agrees that giving a great benefit is forbidden, as we learned in the Mishna (Avoda Zara 47a): \"One whose wall was adjacent to idolatry and it fell, it is forbidden to rebuild it. What should he do? He moves back four amot within his own property and builds….\" And they explained in the Gemara (47b) that he should make a lavatory in the remaining space so as not to give benefit to the idolatry. So too it is ruled in SA YD 143:1. In practice, the halakha follows Rabbi Yoḥanan, that even giving a small benefit is forbidden (Or Zaru'a vol. 4, Avoda Zara 124). According to the Ritva (12b) and the Maharsha (13a), giving benefit to idolatry is forbidden by Torah law, and according to the Mabit (Hilkhot Avoda Zara, chapters 8-9) it is rabbinic."},{"n":7,"en":"Responsa Dor Revi'i (vol. 2, 89:3) forbade selling land for a church to be built on it, because the land is what supports it and is considered an accessory of idolatry. So writes Ḥazon Naḥum (1:62). Likewise, it is forbidden to lend money for the construction of a church, as the SA (YD 139:15) writes in the name of \"there is one who says\" that it is forbidden to lend for the needs of idols (Responsa Ḥayim Bi-yad 28). It seems that when there is a great need, even according to their view it is possible to sell the land to another gentile, and he may sell it to the church (based on SA YD 151:1). So writes Responsa Or Ha-me'ir (6), but he adds that it is preferable to keep away from what is unseemly and from anything resembling it. Likewise, Mareh Ha-bazak (7:18), regarding selling a synagogue to a church, rules that in a pressing situation, when there are no longer Jewish worshippers there, and money is needed to maintain the community and the active synagogue, it is right to permit it by having the community leaders sell it to a gentile broker, who will sell it to whomever he wishes (and see Igrot Moshe OḤ 2:44). According to those who hold that it is permitted to donate to the construction of a church (below 12:9), there is room to say that it would be permitted to sell them land outside the Land for this purpose."}]},{"slug":"20-11-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-07/","title_he":"ז – שימוש בנכסים השייכים לעבודה זרה","title_en":"Using Property Belonging to Idolatry","body_en":"<p>If a house of idolatry had a bathhouse, a museum, apartments or shops for rent: if the payment they collect is designated for the needs of the idolatry itself, it is forbidden to pay them, because of the prohibition of giving benefit to idolatry. But if the payment is designated for the needs of the community associated with that idolatry, it is permitted to enter there for payment. Likewise, it is permitted to enter there free of charge when there is no need to thank the priests for the entry.</p><p>If the payment is designated for the upkeep of the priests: if the building one is paying for is adjacent to the house of idolatry, it is forbidden to pay, since it appears that the payment goes to the idolatry itself (Avoda Zara 51b; SA YD 143:3, 5). But if the building is far from the house of idolatry, then even when the payment goes to the upkeep of the priests it is permitted to pay them, since it does not appear to be giving benefit to the idolatry itself (Rema YD 143:3).<sup>8</sup></p><p>If a bathhouse was the private property of a priest of idolatry, it is permitted to pay him in order to bathe, since this fee is considered his personal profit and not a payment for the maintenance of the idolatry or its worship (Rashba 51b, and Ritva in the name of Rabbeinu Yona). Similarly, it is permitted to rent from a priest a shop or an apartment that belongs to him.</p><p>It is forbidden to insure idols, or even their accessories and ornaments, because they are forbidden for benefit, and by receiving insurance premiums for them one benefits from their existence (see Avoda Zara 63b; SA YD 133:6). If the person requesting the insurance is a decent gentile whom it would not be right to offend, or when there is concern about hostility, one should mediate between him and a gentile who will insure him, and take care not to receive a commission for the mediation (see Har Tzvi YD 117).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"There is no hekdesh (consecration) for idolatry, and therefore there is no prohibition of benefit from property belonging to idolatry. But the Sages said in the Mishna (Avoda Zara 51b): \"One may benefit from them not be-tova, but one may not benefit from them be-tova,\" and the Gemara explains that this means not with the favour of the priests. According to many Rishonim, \"be-tova\" means for payment (Rashi, R. Yehonatan of Lunel, Meiri, Ritva and others), because through one's payment the priests who perform the worship are maintained (Rabbeinu Yona, Or Zaru'a), and perhaps because of it the priests will give thanks to the idolatry (R. Yehonatan of Lunel). Some say that \"be-tova\" means with an expression of gratitude (Rabbeinu Tam and Ra'avad). And some forbid both for payment and with gratitude (Rambam, Ra'ah, Nimukei Yosef and Ran). And so ruled the Aḥaronim (Be'er Ha-gola on SA YD 143:3; Taz 5).<br>On the basis of the reasons that forbid paying the priests or expressing gratitude to the priests, some say that it is forbidden even in a place far from the house of idolatry (Rabbeinu Yona and Meiri; and so is implied by the Or Zaru'a and R. Yehonatan of Lunel). However, according to many, the prohibition applies only near the place of the idolatry, for in truth there is no prohibition to pay the priests or to be grateful to them; rather, near the place of the idolatry it appears that the payment or the gratitude is for the idolatry itself, and therefore it is forbidden (according to this, the additional reasons mentioned for the prohibition serve only as further explanation), whereas at a distance, where it is evident that the gratitude is to the priests, it is permitted (Rabbeinu Tam; Ritva in the name of the Ra'ah; Rosh; and Rema YD 143:3. The Shakh (7) writes that it is right to be stringent like Rabbeinu Yona when, if the Jew does not pay, no gentile will use it in his place and pay).<br>It is forbidden to borrow money at interest from an idolatrous institution, because by paying the interest one gives benefit to the idolatry (Responsa Ḥayim Bi-yad 28)."}]},{"slug":"20-11-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-08/","title_he":"ח – בנייה ותיקונים בבית עבודה זרה","title_en":"Building and Repairs in a House of Idolatry","body_en":"<p>In addition to the Torah prohibition against making an image for idolatry (above 3:3), it is also forbidden to build something that serves idolatry or to add ornamentation to it. Therefore, it is forbidden to build a platform on which an idolatrous image is placed; likewise it is forbidden to build a room or hall in which idolatry is placed, and likewise it is forbidden to install its windows, both because they serve as a shelter for the idolatry and because they are an ornament to it. Likewise, it is forbidden for a Jewish contractor to carry out these works through his gentile workers, since they carry out the work at his instruction. But it is permitted for a Jew to build the rooms and entrance halls in front of the hall in which the idolatry stands, and likewise it is permitted to decorate them, since they are tashmish de-tashmish (an accessory of an accessory) and noy de-noy (an ornament of an ornament). Since Christianity is idolatry for Jews, a church is included in this as well: the place where they bow to the cross is tashmish avoda zara (an accessory of idolatry), and the other rooms are tashmish de-tashmish (SA YD 143:2; Shakh 1).</p><p>After the house of idolatry has already been built, it is forbidden to enter it even to make repairs to a tashmish de-tashmish, because one who enters there appears to be entering for worship. But if he is dressed as a workman and comes with another Jewish worker, there is no suspicion, and he may carry out repairs in rooms that are not used for worship (see Maharsham 3:72). However, ideally it is better for a Jew to keep away even from building or renovating a tashmish de-tashmish or noy de-noy of idolatry.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Mishna Avoda Zara 16a: \"One may build with them basilicas and bathhouses. Once one reaches the dome in which they set up idolatry, it is forbidden to build.\" According to the Ri and the Rashbam in Tosafot (Avoda Zara 16b, s.v. \"Bimosa'ot\"), the Ritva, the Ra'ah and the Taz (4), even a tashmish de-tashmish is forbidden in a building designated for idolatry, and the Mishna permitted building secular structures in which they make a dome for idolatrous worship, which it is forbidden to make. However, according to Rashi (Avoda Zara 16a), the Rambam (9:11), the Rashba and the Ran, the Mishna speaks of a building designated for idolatry, and the prohibition is to build the dome, because it is an accessory of idolatry, whereas the basilica is a tashmish de-tashmish and is therefore permitted; and so it is ruled in SA YD 143:2. They further disagreed on the definition of tashmish de-tashmish: according to the Rashba and the Baḥ (143:1), the house in which the idolatry stands is considered tashmish de-tashmish and is permitted; according to the Shakh (143:1), it is considered tashmish and is forbidden, and so writes the Issur Ve-heter (154:11), and others. Responsa Ein Yitzḥak (vol. 1, OḤ 10) writes that the dispute over the law of tashmish de-tashmish is considered a doubt concerning Torah law, and is to be treated stringently; according to the Minḥat Shai (82), it is considered a doubt concerning rabbinic law. According to the Maharam Schick (YD 153), if one's intention is to honour the idolatry, the prohibition is by Torah law, and if it is to earn a livelihood, the prohibition is because of lifnei iver, which is by Torah law, or because of \"assisting,\" which is rabbinic. In practice, the halakha follows those who are lenient to build a tashmish de-tashmish; on the other hand, the hall in which the idolatry stands, including the floor, the roof and the windows, is considered tashmish and noy of idolatry, and it is forbidden to build them (so writes Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at, first edition, 194; Maharam Schick YD 153).<br>Whatever is forbidden for a Jew is rabbinically forbidden even when the Jew is the contractor and the workers are gentiles, because of amira le-nokhri (instructing a gentile), since the Jew is responsible for the construction. So write the Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at, Maharam Schick and Minḥat Shai cited above. However, with regard to things about which there is a dispute whether they are considered tashmish or tashmish de-tashmish, such as paving the floor of the room in which the worship takes place, one may be lenient and build because of a sfek sfeika (double doubt): a doubt whether it is a tashmish, which is forbidden, and a doubt whether instructing a gentile is forbidden in this case (see Peninei Halakha, Shevi'it 5, n. 9; and see Maharsham 3:72). It seems that in a pressing situation, when a Jew is asked to build an accessory of idolatry, in order to prevent hostility or the loss of a client, the Jew may act as an intermediary and assist in the planning, but the full responsibility will rest on the gentile worker, and the Jew will explain to the one commissioning the work that he cannot be responsible for the work for religious reasons.<br>Igrot Moshe (YD 1:68) was lenient to prepare an architectural plan even for outright idolatry, because he is only drawing a plan, which is in the category of assistance that has no substance; and in addition, according to many the building itself is considered tashmish de-tashmish. End quote. According to the Minḥat Yitzḥak (Likkutei Teshuvot 70), this involves a prohibition of lifnei iver or of assisting in a transgression.<br><b>Benefiting from a tashmish de-tashmish</b>: According to the Ḥatam Sofer (YD 335), it is forbidden to benefit even from a tashmish de-tashmish of idolatry. That is, even according to those who permit building a tashmish de-tashmish, once it has been built and there is already idolatry there, it is forbidden to benefit from it (see Tevu'ot Shor 10:3). On the other hand, according to the Imrei Yosher (1:76), even those who forbid building a tashmish de-tashmish agree that it is permitted to benefit from it. And so writes the Ein Yitzḥak (vol. 1, OḤ 10)."}]},{"slug":"20-11-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-11-09/","title_he":"ט – השכרת בית לעובד עבודה זרה","title_en":"Renting a House to an Idolater","body_en":"<p>The Sages forbade renting a dwelling to an idolater, because he customarily sets up an idolatrous image in his house, and they based their words on what is stated: \"You shall not bring an abomination into your house\" (Devarim 7:26). That is, the Torah prohibition is that a Jew should bring idolatry into his house, and the Sages added and forbade renting a house to a gentile who customarily sets up an idolatrous image in his house; but they did not forbid renting to one who occasionally brings it into his house on a temporary basis (Mishna Avoda Zara 21a; Tosafot, s.v. \"Af\"; SA YD 151:10).</p><p>There is no prohibition against hosting in one's home a Christian who wears a cross around his neck, for the main halakhic view is that it is a symbol and not an image that is worshipped (below 12:12).</p><p>When there is a need for the help of a Christian or Hindu \"foreign worker\" who will live in one of the rooms of the house and care for an elderly person or a patient requiring nursing care, one should agree with him that he will not bring into his room a cross or a Hindu statuette in order to bow to it. In a pressing situation, when it is not possible to agree on this and there is no choice but to rely on his help, one should rent the room to him, and he should remove the mezuza from it until the end of the period in which he lives in the house.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"In the Mishna, Avoda Zara 21a, it is explained that it is forbidden to rent a dwelling to a gentile, \"because he brings idolatry into it, as it is stated: 'You shall not bring an abomination into your house.'\" According to the Yere'im (364), the prohibition is by Torah law, because it is likely that the gentile will bring idolatry into the dwelling, and other Rishonim also raised this possibility (Or Zaru'a 4, Avoda Zara 137; Tosafot Rabbeinu Elḥanan Avoda Zara 21a). However, according to the vast majority of the Rishonim, the Torah prohibition is that a Jew should bring idolatry into his house or agree to its being brought in, and the Sages made a protective distance and forbade renting a house to a gentile when it is likely that he will bring idolatry into the house.<br>In practice, on the basis of various reasons, in the period of the Rishonim the custom was to rent houses to gentiles. Some say that the permission is because in practice the gentiles used to bring idolatry into their houses only on a temporary basis, mainly when they were close to death. Such houses are like storehouses and inns, about which the Tosefta (Avoda Zara 2:9) says that it is permitted to rent them to a gentile even though it is known that the gentile brings idolatry into them, and they explained that the permission is because the idolatry is brought in on a temporary basis (Tosafot Rash Avoda Zara 21a, s.v. \"Af\"; Rosh Avoda Zara 1:22; Nimukei Yosef Avoda Zara 20b. Similarly write the Ra'avan, Avoda Zara 291; Meiri, Avoda Zara 21a; and Tur YD 151:10).<br>Many wrote that outside the Land the Sages did not decree against renting a house to a gentile (Tosafot Rabbeinu Elḥanan Avoda Zara 21a, s.v. \"Af\"; and so write Tosafot ad loc. in his name; Terumah, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 144; and others). The Rashba (Avoda Zara 21a) added that because of the sanctity of the Land of Israel they decreed only in the Land and in Syria. The Ran explained (in his Ḥiddushim ad loc., and on Avoda Zara 6b in the Rif pagination) that they decreed only in the Land, because only there is there a mitzva to root out idolatry. Many Rishonim also mention the reasoning of R. Ḥayim Kohen, that the Torah prohibition \"You shall not bring an abomination into your house\" applies only in the Land, and therefore outside the Land they did not decree against renting. Some explained that the permission is because under gentile law the tenant's acquisition is a complete acquisition, and during the rental period the house is not considered the Jew's (Rosh Avoda Zara 1:22; Nimukei Yosef Avoda Zara 20b, s.v. \"Matnitin\"). The Ra'avan (Avoda Zara 290) added the reasoning that outside the Land the houses are only leased to Jews and are not theirs. The Or Zaru'a (4, Avoda Zara 137) writes that since the Yerushalmi (Avoda Zara 1:10) explains that \"in a place where it is customary to sell, one may sell him even a dwelling and rent him even a dwelling,\" everything depends on custom; and although we do not know clearly what the Yerushalmi's reason is, one may rely on it to be lenient in our places. However, according to the Ramban (Avoda Zara 21a), although the custom is to be lenient in this, \"a ba'al nefesh should refrain from renting even outside the Land.\"<br>As halakha, the SA (YD 151:10) ruled that it is forbidden to rent a dwelling to an idolater, and the Rema there noted that the custom is to be lenient because gentiles do not customarily bring idolatry into their houses. According to the Shakh (ibid. 17), even when gentiles do customarily bring idolatry into their houses, the custom is to rent to them, since one relies on the additional reasons mentioned by the Rishonim.<br>But in the Land, one should be stringent not to rent a house to an idolater, since most of the reasons given by the Rishonim for permitting renting a house to a gentile outside the Land do not apply in the Land of Israel (Responsa Yesh Me-ayin 17, by Rav Eliyahu Yaluz; Divrei Yatziv 3:42). But it is permitted to rent to a Muslim, who is not an idolater (Kaftor Va-feraḥ, chapter 10; Pe'at Ha-shulḥan, Hilkhot Eretz Yisrael 1:19). Some permitted renting even to Christians who bring in a large cross to which they bow, since the prohibition is rabbinic, and some hold that there is no prohibition where there is a monetary loss, and the custom to be lenient has become widespread. So write Responsa Pnei Yitzḥak (vol. 5, YD 1) and Responsa Yisa Ish (YD 2). Rav Makhluf Eldaudi, cited in Yisa Ish there, permitted it with Christians also on the basis of those who hold that they are not considered idolaters at all (above 5:12). The Tzitz Eliezer (22:74) added, based on the Shakh (151:17), another explanation for the custom to be lenient that the Rema wrote: nowadays it is impossible to evict the tenant from the house, and therefore his status is like that of a buyer.<br>Therefore, in a pressing situation, for example when no foreign worker can be found to care for a patient requiring nursing care except one for whom it is important to keep in his room a cross or a Hindu statuette to which he bows, one should rent him the room and refrain from entering it without the foreign worker's permission. And since the room belongs to the gentile, and there is also an image in it, one should remove the mezuza from it."}]}]});
